### Truck Visit Management API — a full visit, end to end
#
# Open this file in VS Code (REST Client extension) or Rider and click "Send Request" above each
# block, top to bottom. It walks one truck through its whole lifecycle, then tries the things that
# should be refused, and finishes by verifying the audit trail.
#
# Setup — two commands:
#   docker compose up -d postgres
#   dotnet run --project src/TruckVisit.Api
#
# Then issue yourself a token and paste it below:
#   dotnet user-jwts create --project src/TruckVisit.Api --name operator-1 --claim terminal=DOVER
#
# Every request is authenticated. There is no anonymous path into this API other than the health
# probes, which is the point of the fallback authorization policy.

@host = http://localhost:5080
@token = PASTE_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE


### 1 — Register a visit
#
# Note what goes in versus what comes back: "mscu 123 4567" and "34 abc 123" are stored as
# MSCU1234567 and 34ABC123. Normalisation happens in the value object's constructor, so no
# endpoint can forget it.
#
# The Idempotency-Key is what a gate device would send. Run this request twice: the second time
# you get 200 instead of 201 and the same visit id, because one arrival must not become two
# records in an audit trail.
#
# @name register
POST {{host}}/api/visits
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: gate-3-20260921-0001

{
  "terminalId": "dover",
  "truck": {
    "unitNumber": "mscu 123 4567",
    "licensePlate": "34 abc 123"
  },
  "driver": {
    "fullName": "Ada Lovelace",
    "documentId": "A1234567",
    "companyName": "Lovelace Haulage Ltd",
    "phoneNumber": "+905001234567"
  },
  "movements": [
    { "type": "Delivery",   "unitNumber": "MSCU1234567", "from": "depot a", "to": "yard 1" },
    { "type": "Collection", "unitNumber": "TGHU7654321", "from": "yard 3",  "to": "depot b" }
  ]
}

@visitId = {{register.response.body.id}}
@firstMovementId = {{register.response.body.movements[0].id}}
@secondMovementId = {{register.response.body.movements[1].id}}


### 2 — Try to skip a step: PreRegistered straight to OnSite
#
# Expect 409. A truck cannot be on site before it has been processed at the gate, and nothing is
# written when a transition is refused — check the audit trail afterwards and it is unchanged.
PATCH {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "OnSite" }


### 3 — The truck arrives at the gate
PATCH {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "AtGate", "reason": "Arrived 20 minutes ahead of slot." }


### 4 — Admitted to the yard
PATCH {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "OnSite" }


### 5 — Try to leave with the work undone
#
# Expect 409. This is the rule the case does not ask for: a truck does not leave until the work it
# came for is done. Without it the record could show a completed visit whose cargo was never
# moved, and nothing in the system would notice.
PATCH {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "Completed" }


### 6 — Record the delivery
POST {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/movements/{{firstMovementId}}/completion
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 7 — Record the collection
#
# hasOutstandingMovements flips to false in the response once both are done.
POST {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/movements/{{secondMovementId}}/completion
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 8 — Now the truck can leave
PATCH {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "Completed" }


### 9 — Try to change a completed visit
#
# Expect 409. Completed is terminal.
PATCH {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/status
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "OnSite" }


### 10 — Read the visit with its full audit trail
#
# Four entries: registration, and the three transitions. Each carries entryHash and previousHash —
# entry N's previousHash equals entry N-1's entryHash. The hashes are published so an auditor can
# verify the chain with their own tooling rather than trusting this service's answer.
GET {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 11 — Verify the audit trail
#
# Expect isIntact: true. The interesting case is when it is false — see the integration tests,
# which disable the database trigger, edit a row directly, re-enable it, and assert that this
# endpoint still reports exactly where the chain broke.
GET {{host}}/api/visits/{{visitId}}/audit/verification
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 12 — Search: everything at this terminal
GET {{host}}/api/visits?terminalId=DOVER&page=1&pageSize=25
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 13 — Search: the gate worklist
#
# Trucks on site whose cargo has not been dealt with. This is the query a gate screen polls, and
# the reason movement completion is modelled at all.
GET {{host}}/api/visits?terminalId=DOVER&currentStatus=OnSite&hasOutstandingMovements=true
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 14 — Search: by movement origin
#
# "yard 1" matches rows stored as YARD1 — the filter is normalised through the same value object
# the data was written with.
GET {{host}}/api/visits?terminalId=DOVER&movementFrom=yard 1
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 15 — Search: a terminal you do not hold
#
# Expect 403. Not an empty page: to an operator whose token was misconfigured, "no results" reads
# as "no trucks today", which is a far more dangerous answer than an error.
GET {{host}}/api/visits?terminalId=HARWICH
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 16 — Read a visit at a terminal you do not hold
#
# Expect 404, not 403. Distinguishing the two would turn the endpoint into an oracle: anyone with
# a valid token could enumerate identifiers and learn which visits exist elsewhere in the network.
# Use an id from another terminal, or any random guid — the answer is deliberately identical.
GET {{host}}/api/visits/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 17 — Validation: a blank plate
#
# Expect 400 with a problem document naming the field. Validation lives in the domain, so the same
# rule produces the same answer no matter which endpoint the value arrived through.
POST {{host}}/api/visits
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "terminalId": "DOVER",
  "truck": { "unitNumber": "MSCU1234567", "licensePlate": "   " },
  "driver": { "fullName": "Ada Lovelace", "documentId": "A1234567", "companyName": "Lovelace Haulage Ltd" },
  "movements": [ { "type": "Delivery", "unitNumber": "MSCU1234567", "from": "YARD1", "to": "BERTH3" } ]
}


### 18 — Validation: a visit with no declared work
#
# Expect 400. A truck admitted with no recorded purpose is a hole in the gate record.
POST {{host}}/api/visits
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "terminalId": "DOVER",
  "truck": { "unitNumber": "MSCU1234567", "licensePlate": "34ABC123" },
  "driver": { "fullName": "Ada Lovelace", "documentId": "A1234567", "companyName": "Lovelace Haulage Ltd" },
  "movements": []
}


### 19 — No token at all
#
# Expect 401. Endpoints are protected by a fallback policy rather than per-route attributes, so a
# route added tomorrow is protected by default rather than by the author remembering.
GET {{host}}/api/visits?terminalId=DOVER


### 20 — Health probes (anonymous by design)
GET {{host}}/health/live

###
GET {{host}}/health/ready
